      "lessonKey": "capstone",
      "parentSampleId": "capstone-01",
      "output": "Verified delay usually means a delay that actually shows up in your production schedule, not just any supplier report. If a supplier says they might be late but you have buffer stock or can reroute, it does not hit you the same way. The ones that matter are delays that force you to stop a line, push back a shipment to a customer, or burn through safety stock. That distinction matters because your prompt needs to know which delays to flag as high-priority and which ones your team can absorb. When you build the prompt, you will need to tell the AI what data counts as proof of a real delay versus noise.",
